1: # CLAUDE.md — SculptCore Blender Addon
2: 
3: Guidance for working in this repository.
4: 
5: ## Debugging
6: 
7: - **Maintain a running debugging guide/lessons-learned in claudeMemory/debugging.md**
8: 
9: ## What this repo is
10: 
11: The **SculptCore sculpt mode** for Blender, shipped as an addon. It registers
12: a first-class object mode (`bpy.types.ObjectModeType`, `bl_idname
13: "sculptcore.sculpt"`) with real enter/exit lifecycle, wrapped undo, and an
14: external draw path — all on top of the custom-mode API that a companion
15: Blender fork provides. The sculpting itself runs in the native **SculptCore
16: engine**, loaded at runtime through a ctypes package that wraps
17: `sculptcore_capi.dll`.
18: 
19: ### Three-repo topology
20: 
21: This addon is one of three coupled repositories:
22: 
23: - **Blender fork** — branch `custom-object-modes` (in the sibling Blender
24:   checkout, e.g. `C:\dev\blender\main`). Carries only the engine-agnostic core
25:   changes that make Python-registered custom object modes possible:
26:   `OB_MODE_CUSTOM`, the `bpy.types.ObjectModeType` RNA type, custom-mode undo,
27:   the external draw provider hooks, the multires reshape API,
28:   `Object.custom_mode_state` (a counter a custom mode bumps when it rebuilds
29:   its live state from the object's data, so it can tell an undo step whose
30:   data it already mirrors from one whose data it does not — see
31:   `sculptcore_addon/handlers.py::_resync_foreign_states`),
32:   `Object.multires_grid_vert_indices` (which grid sample is which subdivided
33:   vertex — the pairing every reshape walk computes internally and none exposed;
34:   see [claudeMemory/research/grid-correspondence.md](./claudeMemory/research/grid-correspondence.md)),
35:   and `Mesh.vertex_group_data_get`/`_set` (bulk CSR read/write of the whole
36:   `MDeformVert` table — the addon's vertex-group bridge would otherwise be a
37:   Python loop over every vertex and influence), the vetoable
38:   `bpy.app.handlers.quit_pre` callback (a handler returning true cancels the
39:   quit — the only hook that runs while a quit can still be stopped) and
40:   `bpy.ops.brush.asset_save_all()` (every other brush-asset operator saves the
41:   *active* brush only). It knows nothing about SculptCore. A stock Blender without these changes cannot load this addon's
42:   mode.
43: - **This repo** (`sculptcore-blender-addon`) — the addon Python
44:   (`sculptcore_addon/`) plus the engine as a submodule (`engine/`) and the
45:   build tooling that ties them to a Blender build.
46: - **Engine** (`engine/`, submodule → `joeedh/sculptcore.git`) — the C++
47:   sculpting engine. Built and documented on its own; see
48:   [engine/CLAUDE.md](./engine/CLAUDE.md).
49: 
50: ## Layout
51: 
52: ```
53: sculptcore_addon/        The addon package Blender loads (bl_info; registers the mode).
54:   __init__.py            SculptCoreMode(ObjectModeType) + register()/unregister().
55:   engine.py              Single load point for the `sculptcore` ctypes package + DLL.
56:   convert.py             Mesh <-> engine conversion (enter/exit/flush/refresh).
57:   stroke.py, ops.py ...  Stroke operator, brush mapping, gestures, undo, UI, keymap.
58:   texture.py             Brush-texture bridge: 2D bakes, runtime .stex programs, samplers.
59:   stex/                  Texture scripts the engine compiles at runtime (clouds.stex);
60:                          3D-mapped procedurals route here instead of the 2D bake.
61:                          See engine/documentation/textureScripts.md.
62:   lib/                   Vendored engine runtime (ctypes pkg + DLLs). Build product; gitignored.
63: brush_save_reminder/     A second, standalone addon (shares nothing with the above): warns on
64:                          quit about brush assets with unsaved changes, which neither the file
65:                          save nor Blender's own quit prompt covers. Needs two fork additions —
66:                          `bpy.app.handlers.quit_pre` and `bpy.ops.brush.asset_save_all()`.
67:                          See brush_save_reminder/README.md.
68: brushes/                 Addon-authored .sbrush kernels compiled into the DLL at build
69:                          time (engine "extra kernel dirs"); see brushes/README.md.
70: engine/                  SculptCore engine (git submodule). Builds sculptcore_capi.dll.
71: tools/                   build-blender-dist.mjs  build Blender locally, stage the addon (below).
72:                          fetch-blender-dist.mjs  the same, from CI artifacts instead of a build.
73:                          verify_addon.py         headless "did it come up enabled?" check.
74:                          smoke_test_package.py   headless "does its engine load?" check.
75:                          record-release.mjs      builds-repo manifest + RELEASES.md index.
76: .github/workflows/       Packaging CI (build-packages.yml) + the package smoke test
77:                          (smoke-test-packages.yml) — see below.
78: claudeMemory/            Claude's plans, research, and validated reference notes for THIS repo.
79: ```
80: 
81: ## How the engine reaches the addon
82: 
83: The addon does **not** compile into `blender.exe`. A change to the C++ engine
84: (`engine/source/**`) reaches Blender by **rebuilding the DLL and re-vendoring**
85: it into `sculptcore_addon/lib/`; a change to the addon Python needs nothing
86: rebuilt. Build the DLL with the engine's own dispatcher (run inside `engine/`):
87: 
88: ```
89: cd engine
90: node make.mjs build python     # builds sculptcore_capi.dll + wgpu_native.dll under engine/build/python/
91: ```
92: 
93: Then the runtime (the `sculptcore` ctypes package + those DLLs) is vendored
94: into `sculptcore_addon/lib/sculptcore/`. The build/install helper (below)
95: performs this vendoring against a Blender install tree; see
96: `engine/CLAUDE.md` for the engine's own `make.mjs bundle` target.
97: 
98: **Discovery** (see `sculptcore_addon/engine.py` and the package's `_capi.py`):
99: - The `sculptcore` package is found via, in order: an already-importable
100:   `sculptcore`; `$SCULPTCORE_PYTHON_PATH` (a dev checkout, which wins); or the
101:   vendored `sculptcore_addon/lib/sculptcore/`.
102: - The DLL is found via, in order: `$SCULPTCORE_CAPI_PATH`; a copy beside the
103:   package (the vendored case; `wgpu_native.dll` resolves via
104:   `add_dll_directory` on that directory); or `engine/build/python/`.
105: 
106: To iterate on the engine without touching the vendored copy:
107: 
108: ```
109: $env:SCULPTCORE_PYTHON_PATH = "C:\dev\blender\sculptcore-blender-addon\engine\python"
110: $env:PATH = "C:\dev\blender\sculptcore-blender-addon\engine\build\python;$env:PATH"
111: ```
112: 
113: `engine.py`'s `init()` refuses an ABI-mismatched DLL; on any load failure the
114: addon reports it to the system console (Window → Toggle System Console).
115: 
116: ## The build/install helper (`tools/build-blender-dist.mjs`)
117: 
118: One command assembles a runnable Blender with the sculpt mode bundled and
119: **enabled by default**:
120: 
121: ```
122: node tools/build-blender-dist.mjs [--build-dir DIR] [--dist DIR] [--config CFG]
123:                                   [--skip-blender] [--skip-engine] [--run]
124: ```
125: 
126: The chain (`node tools/build-blender-dist.mjs --help` for the full option list):
127: 
128: 1. Build the Blender fork's `install` target (its Windows `bin/` tree is a
129:    portable Blender). Skipped with `--skip-blender`. The build tree is
130:    autodetected as `../build_*_<config>` beside the fork (`../main`), or passed
131:    with `--build-dir`.
132: 2. Pick the install folder: a clean mirror at `--dist DIR`, or the build's
133:    `bin/` in place (default, fast for dev).
134: 3. Copy `sculptcore_addon/` and `brush_save_reminder/` into
135:    `<install>/<ver>/scripts/addons_core/` (fresh; `lib/` excluded). Blender 5.x only scans `scripts/addons_core` in an install
136:    tree, not the legacy `scripts/addons`.
137: 4. Vendor the engine runtime into the addon's `lib/` via the engine's own
138:    `node make.mjs bundle <lib> ` (builds the DLL too; `--skip-engine` restages
139:    existing outputs only).
140: 5. Write `<install>/<ver>/scripts/addons_core/.always_enable` (one module name
141:    per line — both addons) so the mode is on at startup, then verify it headlessly
142:    (`--background --factory-startup --python tools/verify_addon.py`).
143: 
144:    **Enabled-by-default without owning the user config.** The fork's
145:    `scripts/modules/addon_utils.py` reads `.always_enable` from each add-on
146:    directory in `_initialize_once()` and folds those names into
147:    `_addons_hidden_core`, which is enabled with `default_set=False` (never
148:    written to `preferences.addons`, so no `userpref.blend` is touched) and
149:    `persistent=True` (`check()` then reports it as enabled-by-default, so
150:    `reset_all()` will not unload it on a preferences reload). Timing matters:
151:    this runs in `load_scripts_extensions()`, *outside* the `RestrictBlend` block
152:    that `scripts/startup` modules register under — the addon's
153:    `keymap.register()` needs a live `bpy.context.window_manager`. Being
154:    hidden-core also means the addon does not appear in the Add-ons list and is
155:    exempt from per-workspace add-on filtering. It is enabled under
156:    `--factory-startup` too, which is what makes the verify pass meaningful.
157: 
158:    The install therefore ships **no user config**: it reads and writes the
159:    machine's global Blender config like any other install. Do not create a
160:    directory named `portable` beside the executable — Blender 5.x treats that
161:    alone as the portable marker (`get_path_user_ex()` in `appdir.cc`) and
162:    redirects *all* user resources under `<base>/portable/<folder>`, which is
163:    exactly what this no longer wants. `build-blender-dist.mjs` deletes a stale
164:    one left by earlier revisions when staging in place.
165: 
166: The fork's side of this is engine-agnostic (it names no add-on); the
167: `.always_enable` marker and the vendored `lib/` are build products, never
168: committed. For tight engine iteration without a full restage, use the env-var
169: flow above pointed at a Blender fork build.
170: 
171: Prerequisites for step 4 are the engine's own (Node + CMake + toolchain; see
172: `engine/CLAUDE.md`). The script has no npm dependencies.
173: 
174: ## Packaging CI (`.github/workflows/build-packages.yml`)
175: 
176: The shippable, downloadable builds. Manual dispatch only. One matrix job per
177: target OS (ubuntu / macos-arm64 / windows), and each job **is** the target OS,
178: so `tools/fetch-blender-dist.mjs` takes its host path — extract, stage, mark
179: always-enabled, and verify by actually running Blender — and the final
180: tar.gz/zip is packed natively, preserving exec bits and symlinks.
181: 
182: **It is the second of two workflows.** The fork's own `build.yml` (manual
183: dispatch on `custom-object-modes`, ~2.5 h) builds all three platforms and uploads
184: `blender-install-<OS>`; on Linux/macOS that is a single *uncompressed* tar,
185: because the artifact store re-zips everything and a zip carries no exec bits or
186: symlinks. Run it first whenever the fork moved, then dispatch packaging with
187: `blender_run` set to that run id — left blank it takes the latest successful run
188: on `blender_branch`, which may predate the fork change being packaged. A fork
189: build without the `.always_enable` support fails packaging at the verify step
190: rather than shipping a Blender whose sculpt mode never turns on. The other
191: dispatch inputs are `config` (engine build config), `tag` (default
192: `build-<UTC date>-<short sha>`) and `prerelease`.
193: 
194: Per job:
195: 
196: 1. Build the engine libs here, with `node make.mjs bundle ci-staging
197:    --kernels-extra ../brushes`. This is why packaging can't live in the engine
198:    repo: only this repo has the addon's `.sbrush` kernels, and libs built
199:    without them make the addon report `kernel 'NUDGE' missing from engine enum`.
200: 
201:    `--publish-deps-to staged-deps` also exports any *freshly compiled* native
202:    deps combo (OpenBLAS + SuiteSparse/CHOLMOD) as a `deps-<label>-<config>`
203:    artifact — a cold deps cache dominates this job's runtime, so the combo is
204:    worth keeping. Nothing is pushed from the runner: feed the artifact to the
205:    engine's `tools/publish-deps-from-package.mjs --commit --push` to land it in
206:    `joeedh/sculptcore-deps` (the libs go through git-LFS, so publish from a
207:    machine that has it). Combos are keyed
208:    `{platform}/clang-<major>-<arch>/{config}` and only hit on a matching
209:    toolchain — a runner image that bumps clang's major version misses and
210:    re-exports. Worth doing promptly: a warm cache is the difference between a
211:    ~2 h job and a ~7 min one.
212: 2. `fetch-blender-dist.mjs --blender-repo joeedh/blender --engine-libs enginelibs`
213:    — the two CI-only flags. `--engine-libs` also bypasses the ABI pin, which is
214:    safe here because the libs were just built from the same submodule commit
215:    whose ctypes package gets vendored.
216: 
217:    Vendoring also **relinks the engine libs** (`fixLibLinkage()`). The prebuilt
218:    `wgpu_native` has no SONAME, so on Linux `libsculptcore_capi.so` records the
219:    build machine's *absolute path* to it as `DT_NEEDED` — unresolvable anywhere
220:    else, no matter that the lib is colocated. `patchelf` (hence its apt entry)
221:    sets a SONAME, rewrites the `NEEDED`, and sets `$ORIGIN` as the rpath. macOS
222:    is the same bug in milder form — the install name is already `@rpath`-relative
223:    but the only `LC_RPATH` is the runner's `extern/wgpu_native/lib` — fixed with
224:    `install_name_tool` (`-delete_rpath` / `-add_rpath @loader_path`) plus an
225:    ad-hoc `codesign`, since editing load commands voids the signature.
226: 
227:    Windows needs no *relinking* — PE resolves imports by bare name, and ctypes
228:    loads the capi with `LOAD_WITH_ALTERED_SEARCH_PATH`, so the DLL's own
229:    directory wins over System32 — but it does need the dependencies to be
230:    present. The engine is a clang build and imports `libomp140.x86_64.dll`,
231:    which ships with Visual Studio / LLVM and *not* with the VC++
232:    redistributable, so it resolved out of System32 on the builder and was
233:    missing from every package until `tools/lib/windows-deps.mjs` landed: it
234:    parses the PE import table (no `dumpbin` on a runner), copies in every
235:    non-system import — following the copies, which have imports of their own —
236:    and fails the build if one cannot be found. `build-blender-dist.mjs` runs it
237:    too, so a dev install is as self-contained as a package.
238: 
239:    None of this shows up on a dev box, where the recorded build paths and the
240:    toolchain runtimes exist — which is what the package smoke test (below) is
241:    for.
242: 3. Pack, having first renamed `dist/<os>` to the package name, so the archive
243:    holds a single top-level `sculptblender-<tag>-<platform>/` directory —
244:    extracting one must not scatter ~1.5 GB across the user's current directory.
245: 
246:    The archive **file**, unlike that directory, carries no tag:
247:    `sculptblender-<platform>.(tar.gz|zip)`. GitHub's only stable-download URL is
248:    `/releases/latest/download/<asset name>`, which matches by exact name, so a
249:    tagged file name can never be linked to; the tag stays on the inner
250:    directory, where it still keeps two extracted builds from colliding and
251:    remains visible after unpacking. The same rule is why `prerelease` defaults
252:    to **false** — GitHub's "latest" skips pre-releases entirely, and while every
253:    release was one, `/releases/latest` 302'd to the plain releases list.
254: 4. Upload straight into a draft release on `joeedh/sculptblender-builds`
255:    (created by the `prepare` job, un-drafted by `finalize`), so a ~1.5 GB
256:    package crosses the wire once instead of twice through the artifact store.
257: 
258: `finalize` also runs `tools/record-release.mjs` against a clone of the builds
259: repo: binaries stay release assets, and git gets only `releases/<tag>.json` plus
260: a regenerated `RELEASES.md`. The index orders on each manifest's `created_at`
261: timestamp — several builds land on one day, and ordering by date alone fell
262: through to the tag's sha suffix, i.e. alphabetical noise. `--reindex`
263: regenerates `RELEASES.md` from the manifests already on disk, which is how the
264: published index gets repaired without waiting for the next build.
265: 
266: `RELEASES.md` leads with a **Latest build** table of `/releases/latest/download/`
267: links, but only when a manifest on disk actually satisfies both halves of that
268: contract — not a pre-release, and carrying assets under the tag-free names.
269: Releases predating the rename fail the second test, so the section stays absent
270: rather than publishing links that 404.
271: 
272: ## Package smoke test (`.github/workflows/smoke-test-packages.yml`)
273: 
274: Packaging verifies the tree it assembles **on the machine that just built the
275: engine libraries** — where every build path still exists, every toolchain
276: runtime is installed, and nothing has been through a pack/unpack round trip. So
277: it structurally cannot catch a mis-linked library, a lost exec bit, or a
278: dependency satisfied from the builder. This workflow closes that gap: it takes
279: the most recent published release from `joeedh/sculptblender-builds` (or a
280: dispatched `tag`), unpacks it on a clean runner of the target OS, and launches
281: Blender against `tools/verify_addon.py` (enabled?) and
282: `tools/smoke_test_package.py` (does the engine load, and from where?).
283: 
284: A third step, `tools/verify_texture_parity.py`, grades the shipped `.stex`
285: texture programs against Blender's own texture evaluation — the only job where a
286: Blender and the engine coexist, and the copy it grades is the one users get. It
287: also covers what the other two cannot see: a `.stex` that ships in the archive
288: but no longer compiles against the vendored DLL.
289: 
290: Triggered manually, and automatically on a successful **Build packages** run
291: (`workflow_run`) — packaging un-drafts the release in its `finalize` job, so by
292: then the assets are the "most recent".
293: 
294: **It installs nothing an ordinary user would not already have**, and that
295: constraint is the point: a compiler, patchelf, git-lfs or an OpenMP runtime on
296: the test machine would let a package that only loads on a build machine pass.
297: The one exception is the standard X11/GL runtime any Linux desktop carries
298: (Blender links it even under `--background`); a bare CI container has *less*
299: than a user's machine, not more. Unpacking uses only OS-provided tools —
300: including Windows' own `System32\tar.exe`, since the Git bash on PATH there
301: ships GNU tar, which cannot read a zip.
302: 
303: `smoke_test_package.py` asserts provenance, not just success: the `sculptcore`
304: ctypes package and every engine shared library the *process actually mapped*
305: (read from `/proc/self/maps`, `_dyld_get_image_name`, or `EnumProcessModules`)
306: must resolve inside the add-on's vendored `lib/sculptcore/`. A system copy
307: quietly satisfying the load fails the job instead of hiding in it. It also
308: requires `Brush.sculptcore` to exist (proving the engine was live during the
309: add-on's own registration — `engine_props.register()`'s failure path is a
310: `print`, not an exception), every kernel in `mapping.KERNEL_BY_TYPE` to be in
311: the engine enum (how libs built without `--kernels-extra ../brushes` show up),
312: and a one-triangle mesh to round-trip through the c-api. Both scripts are
313: launched with `--python-exit-code 1`: without it Blender prints an unhandled
314: exception and still exits 0.
315: 
316: The builds repo's `Readme.MD` is the user-facing install doc (nothing regenerates
317: it) — it describes the archive layout, the always-enabled add-on and the global
318: config, so a change to any of those means editing it by hand.
319: 
320: Needs the repo secret **`BUILDS_TOKEN`** — a PAT with `actions:read` on
321: `joeedh/blender` (the default `GITHUB_TOKEN` is repo-scoped and cannot download
322: the fork's artifacts) and `contents:write` on `joeedh/sculptblender-builds`.
323: 
324: ## Working conventions for Claude (this repo)
325: 
326: - Put everything Claude generates under `claudeMemory/` (plans → `plans/`,
327:   research → `research/`, validated reference docs → `codebase/`, designs →
328:   `design/`). Index in [claudeMemory/README.md](./claudeMemory/README.md).
329: - Prefix scaffolding/helper comments with `CLAUDENOTE:` so they are greppable;
330:   strip them before a task is considered done, then audit every comment you
331:   touched for accuracy.
332: - **Pressure-test every plan after formulation.** Launch multiple adversarial
333:   agents with fresh contexts (one lens each — per-workstream buildability,
334:   semantics/correctness, cross-cutting seams and gates) whose brief is to
335:   *kill* the plan against the actual code, then fold every surviving finding
336:   back into the plan before any phase starts. A citation audit is not a
337:   substitute: it verifies references, not buildability.
338: 
339: ## Coding style
340: 
341: - **Python** follows Blender's guidelines: PEP 8, 4-space indent, 120-column
342:   lines, `underscore_case` (CamelCase for classes), single quotes for enum
343:   literals (`ob.type == 'MESH'`) and double quotes elsewhere. Prefer
344:   `str.format()` over f-strings in code that may be translated. Imports inside
345:   function bodies are fine (and preferred for startup-cost-sensitive paths).
346: - **Engine (C++)** follows the engine repo's own conventions — see
347:   `engine/CLAUDE.md` / `engine/AGENTS.MD`.
348: - Every new source file needs an SPDX header
349:   (`GPL-2.0-or-later`, `2026 Blender Authors`), matching the addon's existing
350:   files.
