1: # Generic brush contract, version 1
2: 
3: Status: Plan 1 fixture gate passed (2026-09-15); downstream implementation in progress.
4: These are implementation decisions, not additional user requirements.
5: The eight gates in [the task list](../plans/generic-brush-properties-tasks.md)
6: remain mandatory. This contract does not enable the new path.
7: 
8: ## Identity and migration
9: 
10: Saved schema version is 1. Definitions, authored records and immutable resolved
11: records are distinct. IDs are explicit registry strings; labels, collection
12: indices, manifest order, named-float slots and memory addresses are never IDs.
13: Common native settings use `sculptcore.brush.<semantic_name>`; kernel uniforms
14: use `sculptcore.kernel.<kernel_name_lowercase>.<uniform_name>` preserving the
15: uniform's case. Kernel names become permanent registry namespaces: renaming a
16: kernel requires an explicit alias. Only the common native adapter table shares
17: IDs across kernels. For example, `strength` is shared; each kernel's `projection`
18: has a separate ID even if today's C++ storage happens to be the same field.
19: 
20: Freeze the old generated RNA declarations and every kernel/name association
21: from the baseline runtime, including registered defaults/ranges and live Brush
22: defaults. Migration uses that frozen data without querying a new DLL. For each
23: legacy `Brush.sculptcore[name]`, fan the authored value out to every frozen
24: kernel ID associated with that name. Do this even for inactive kernels, so
25: changing brush type later retains the old shared setting. If unset, resolve
26: the frozen *registered RNA default* for all those IDs, not a new DSL default.
27: Keep `authored=false` distinct from explicit set-to-default. Preserve the raw
28: legacy group for rollback; never delete it on read. Unknown names and newer
29: schema records are retained opaquely and cannot be downgraded. Missing kernels
30: disable execution of their records without rewriting values or IDs.
31: 
32: Native values and curves remain authoritative in their native RNA. Migration
33: adds metadata and unmapped storage only. Legacy assets are migrated lazily in
34: memory after activation; persistence requires explicit asset save. Read-only
35: assets expose resolved reads and require Save As for changes. No library scan
36: rewrites assets. Existing native RNA paths stay intact; generated legacy paths
37: need aliases with fan-out writes in Plan 8. Drivers on ambiguous shared legacy
38: paths continue to affect all associated IDs until explicitly migrated.
39: 
40: Legacy alias reads select the active kernel's ID when it owns the name;
41: otherwise return the preserved legacy value/default. Unset clears all fan-out
42: authoring flags and restores their frozen defaults. A legacy animated path is
43: an explicit resolver-time overlay on every associated ID, even after those IDs
44: diverge; removing/muting that animation reveals the independent values. Raw
45: legacy writes are detected by comparing value/presence at synchronization.
46: Use evaluated animation data, not update callbacks, as the overlay source.
47: Freeze union-declaration winning provenance, hard/soft ranges and DLL/source
48: identity alongside manifest associations.
49: 
50: ## Ownership and resolution
51: 
52: Value modes: `UNIFIED` (default), `ALWAYS`, `NEVER`. Scene per-property unified
53: defaults to false, except native size/strength use the current native flag.
54: Scene is the root. A scene's own value never inherits. Stacks have the simpler
55: `inherit_stack` boolean, default false for every brush. This preserves native
56: unified values with brush-local native or shadow pressure controls.
57: 
58: Compatibility policy for the initial rollout is `LEGACY_SCENE`: preserve the
59: deprecated UnifiedPaintSettings flags read by today's SculptCore. The fork's
60: new Brush.use_unified_* flags are retained but do not silently replace this
61: policy. Generic controls show the effective policy. Conversion to per-brush
62: native-unified behavior is explicit, with a preview of differing values;
63: the user has been asked whether immediate conversion is preferred. Keep the
64: two flags independent until that compatibility choice is settled.
65: 
66: | Brush mode | Scene unified | Value owner | Edit strength .2 local / .8 scene to .6 |
67: | --- | --- | --- | --- |
68: | UNIFIED | false | Brush | local=.6, scene=.8 |
69: | UNIFIED | true | Scene | local=.2, scene=.6 |
70: | ALWAYS | either | Scene | local=.2, scene=.6 |
71: | NEVER | either | Brush | local=.6, scene=.8 |
72: 
73: Changing mode/flags never copies or resets values. With local=.2 and scene=.8,
74: ALWAYS then NEVER restores .2. Stack ownership resolves independently: with
75: ALWAYS and `inherit_stack=false`, rows edit scene values and brush pressure
76: curves; with NEVER and `inherit_stack=true`, they edit brush values and scene
77: stacks. Disabling pressure toggles its unique entry, preserving order, curve,
78: mix and factor. Adding an existing device updates it in place; explicit move
79: changes order. Imported duplicate types are a validation error, preserved for
80: repair rather than silently merged. Empty and fully disabled stacks are identity.
81: 
82: An absent value record uses the definition's default at the selected owner
83: without allocation. An absent stack record is an empty stack at that owner;
84: legacy native pressure adapters supply virtual entries without storing them.
85: If the requested parent lacks a *definition/capability*, resolve locally with
86: an explicit `parent_unavailable` diagnostic and local write target. Do not
87: invent parent records. Unknown local definitions cannot execute. Future parent
88: links must be validated as an acyclic graph before mutation; resolution also
89: detects cycles and fails before any stroke mutation.
90: 
91: Read resolution never allocates saved records or curves. Explicit setters
92: materialize only the selected owner, validate editability/type/range and notify
93: that owner. Radial controls and bracket sizing use these same setters, capture
94: the owner at invocation, and restore its old authored value on cancel. Owner
95: changes during a modal edit cancel it safely. Placement is brush metadata with
96: definition defaults; expansion is WindowManager runtime state. Neither inherits.
97: Positions have unique stable location IDs, each carrying one integer sort index;
98: ties sort by property ID. One property may occupy header, panel and context menu.
99: 
100: Size is a coupled adapter block: pixel diameter, world diameter and locked-size
101: mode share one value owner and inheritance policy. The current native pixel
102: setter rescales its world partner; the current world setter leaves pixels
103: unchanged (its scale helper divides the new value by itself). Preserve that
104: existing asymmetry in authoring adapters. Modal cancel snapshots/restores both values exactly through an
105: atomic native restoration operation (including authored presence where relevant).
106: The new path will honor SCENE size as world diameter/2, transformed to object
107: space, and honor the effective owner on off-screen fallback. These are explicit
108: corrections: the old stroke always pixel-unprojects and falls back to local
109: Brush.unprojected_size. Preserve pixel-mode baselines; test world-mode corrected
110: expectations independently with unequal object scale and paired non-round sizes.
111: The common stack ID is `sculptcore.brush.size`; pixel/world diameter fields
112: are two native representations of that semantic size. Apply its stack once
113: to the projected/effective radius; cursor uses the same evaluated size. Do not
114: attach separate pressure stacks to pixel and world representations.
115: 
116: For strength/size brush-owned pressure entries, enabled always reads the native
117: or shadow flag selected by current native capabilities, even after richer stack
118: metadata exists. CUSTOM always edits/evaluates the native curve; selecting a
119: generated preset preserves it dormant. A script edit to the dormant native
120: curve does not switch the preset; returning to CUSTOM reveals the edit.
121: Changing brush type reselects the native/shadow flag without copying either;
122: stack curve/mix/order metadata survives. Scene-owned pressure uses addon-owned
123: storage because there is no equivalent native scene curve/enable pair.
124: PINCH currently uses local Brush.strength for its `pinch` extra even while
125: unified strength drives the strength uniform. Retain that local-source family
126: adapter for legacy parity; expose the distinction in diagnostics. AIRBRUSH,
127: LINE and CURVE currently use the ordinary spacer; retain this behavior without
128: claiming native airbrush timing or line/curve authoring support. Texture angle,
129: rake/randomization, stencil placement and secondary mask texture are retained
130: native data with unsupported/approximate execution explicitly identified.
131: 
132: ## Compound cavity adapter and automasking
133: 
134: Retain the existing specialized cavity block adapter for migrated data. The
135: block's ownership policy is `NATIVE_CAVITY` until explicitly changed to generic
136: UNIFIED/ALWAYS/NEVER; changing policy never rewrites either native block.
137: All cavity rows share the block policy, including enables, inversion, factor,
138: blur, curve toggle and custom curve. Do not independently inherit those fields.
139: Native precedence is recomputed on each synchronization, including script edits.
140: 
141: Let B/S mean either cavity enable is true on Brush/Paint. Fixtures use local
142: factor=1.75, blur=3, curve endpoint=.375 and scene factor=.625, blur=1,
143: curve endpoint=.875, with custom curves enabled on both.
144: 
145: | B | S | Effective block | factor / blur / endpoint | inversion |
146: | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
147: | false | false | disabled; edit Brush | no cavity effect | false |
148: | false | true | Paint (Scene ID) | .625 / 1 / .875 | scene inverted flag |
149: | true | false | Brush | 1.75 / 3 / .375 | brush inverted flag |
150: | true | true | Brush | 1.75 / 3 / .375 | brush inverted flag |
151: 
152: If both normal and inverted flags are true, inverted wins as in today's bridge.
153: Ordinary native RNA setters mutually exclude these flags; the both-true case is
154: a defensive malformed/raw-legacy case tested through an independent adapter
155: stub, not manufactured by two ordinary RNA assignments.
156: Editing the effective scene enable off can reveal the brush fallback; subsequent
157: rows re-resolve. UI shows the block owner and offers explicit local override.
158: 
159: | Native control | Engine target / defaults | Current bridge | v1 disposition |
160: | --- | --- | --- | --- |
161: | cavity and inverted cavity | automask_cavity=false, cavity_inverted=false | wired block | equivalent enable and inversion |
162: | cavity factor | cavity_factor=1, dimensionless | wired | equivalent remap, retain native authored value |
163: | cavity blur steps | cavity_blur_steps=2, integer neighborhood radius | wired | translated mesh/grid neighborhood estimator; no claim of geometric parity |
164: | custom cavity toggle/curve | cavity_use_curve=false, identity table | wired, 256 samples | equivalent curve-before-invert order; native owned curve |
165: | view normal enable/limit/falloff | automask_view_normal=false; limit=pi/2, falloff=25*pi/180 | not wired | new engine-owned settings, radians; do not adopt native defaults |
166: | front-face / backface controls | cull_backfaces=false | not wired | engine culling is part of view-normal mask; expose with view-normal enable |
167: | view direction | viewDir, object-space eye-to-surface ray | not wired for mask | transient per-dab sample, reflected with symmetry, all mesh/grid/batch paths |
168: | topology, face sets, boundary edges/face sets, propagation steps | no matching bridge | unsupported | omit SculptCore controls; retain native data |
169: | start normal / occlusion / normal falloff | no matching bridge | unsupported | omit; do not relabel as engine view-normal |
170: 
171: Engine view-normal uses abs(dot) when culling is off, so back faces fade like
172: front faces. Factor reaches zero at limit, ramps over falloff radians before
173: limit, and uses a hard cutoff for falloff<=1e-6. Degenerate normals/rays give
174: identity. This differs from native controls; store its settings separately.
175: All additional native automasking RNA fields are unsupported unless this table
176: explicitly maps them. Cavity is stroke-cached; view-normal is evaluated per dab.
177: The full advanced panel and header popover will share SculptCore's renderer;
178: remove `SCULPTCORE_PT_automasking` only after that renderer reaches execution.
179: Plan 2 must also correct native MeshAutomaskingSettings notifications to use
180: ptr.owner_id and mark the owning Brush dirty. Its current callback notifies
181: the active brush and never tags the edited asset. Test inactive-Brush scalar
182: and native curve edits, plus Scene edits leaving all Brush assets clean.
183: Cavity parameters are stroke-static, not device-dynamic in v1. Independent
184: command cavity configurations require separate configuration-keyed caches
185: (stroke generation plus full blur/factor/inversion/curve identity); a vertex's
186: fully remapped factor must not be reused for a different command configuration.
187: 
188: ## Typed dynamics
189: 
190: v1 dynamic storage types are FLOAT32, INT32 and BOOL. Integer enums, bit masks,
191: IDs, vectors, strings, FLOAT64 and other integer widths are not dynamic-capable.
192: Static uniforms are never eligible. Capability is the intersection of type,
193: kernel metadata, execution-path support and delivered inputs; unsupported
194: configuration fails preflight before geometry mutation.
195: 
196: Preserve the authored base; evaluate a separate working value each dab.
197: FLOAT32 uses IEEE float32 operations matching the existing float evaluator.
198: INT32/BOOL arithmetic uses float64 (all int32 bases are exact); convert once
199: after the ordered stack. BOOL begins as 0 or 1. Given value v, response r and
200: mix factor a in [0,1], compute c as r (REPLACE), v*r (MULTIPLY), v+r (ADD),
201: v-r (SUBTRACT), or abs(v-r) (DIFFERENCE), then v=v+(c-v)*a.
202: Disabled/missing-sample entries are skipped. No conversion between layers.
203: 
204: At the end clamp to the declared numeric range intersected with the storage
205: range. INT32 then rounds ties away from zero and saturates before casting;
206: BOOL converts with >=.5 true after clamping to [0,1]. FLOAT32 retains its
207: declared finite range. Empty stacks return the valid authored base exactly.
208: Non-finite bases, factors, parameters and curve samples are rejected at write
209: or upload. A non-finite/missing device sample skips that layer for this dab;
210: no old sample persists. Non-finite intermediate arithmetic abandons that
211: layer and retains its previous finite v. No fast-math reassociation at typed
212: conversion boundaries. CPU/grid/program/batch tests use the same contract.
213: 
214: Independent examples: INT32 base 3, multiply .5 => 2; base -3 => -2;
215: base 3, multiply .5 then add .4 => 2 (conversion only at end); BOOL base true,
216: multiply .49 => false, multiply .5 => true; base false, add .75 => true.
217: INT32 max plus 1 saturates at 2147483647. Disabled ADD yields the original base.
218: 
219: ## Input acquisition and sampling
220: 
221: Pressure domain [0,1], mouse pressure=1. Missing tablet pressure skips pressure
222: for that dab. Tilt X/Y arrive signed [-1,1], normalized as (x+1)/2 for curves;
223: missing tilt skips the respective layer. Tilt is a screen/tablet input, so
224: symmetry copies it unchanged. viewDir is geometric and is reflected instead.
225: ANGLE, CURVATURE and TWIST stay unavailable in v1.
226: 
227: Choose acquisition-time speed, requiring a generic fork timestamp API in
228: Plan 3. `Event.time` is monotonic seconds (double), sourced from GHOST event
229: time through queued/coalesced/INBETWEEN events. Synthetic event APIs accept an
230: explicit time with the same units; absence makes speed unavailable, never
231: handler-time speed. No timestamp is persisted in a brush.
232: The fork extension also preserves an input-presence mask from the device API
233: through GHOST/wmEvent/RNA and synthetic events. Pressure, tilt X and tilt Y
234: have independent validity bits; zero tilt is a valid sample, not absence.
235: Platforms must advertise only channels whose availability they can establish.
236: 
237: Speed is screen pixels/second, normalized by an authored positive
238: `speed_reference` (default 1000 px/s), clamped to [0,1]. First sample speed=0.
239: Each positive-time segment uses distance/delta-time; duplicate or decreasing
240: times yield missing speed and reset the derivative anchor. A pause contributes
241: to the next segment's time, so subsequent movement is slower. New strokes reset
242: all sample history. Motion is measured in region pixels, before symmetry.
243: 
244: Dabs emitted at arc fractions interpolate pressure, tilt and time between
245: the segment's event endpoints; speed is constant on that segment. Missing input
246: at either endpoint skips that input for interpolated dabs. Release flush uses
247: the final event and emits only residual dabs required by the existing spacer;
248: it never reuses a later pressure across an earlier segment. Example: x=0..100,
249: t=0..1, pressure=.2..1: dabs at x=25,50,75 have pressure=.4,.6,.8 and speed=.1
250: with default normalization. Delaying handler delivery changes none of these.
251: Constant-pressure baselines must remain unchanged; old variable-pressure
252: geometry is not an oracle for the corrected sampler.
253: Dab interpolation uses traveled arc length divided by the full segment arc
254: length, not cubic parameter t. Preserve that fraction in the spacer output.
255: Raw, anchored and drag-dot dabs use current-event inputs and speed even when
256: their geometric center stays at the anchor. Test a curved uneven-knot segment
257: in addition to the straight example.
258: 
259: ## Curves and cache contract
260: 
261: Canonical device input x is clamped to [0,1]. Generated responses are:
262: CONSTANT(c)=c (default 1); TWO_STEP(t,lo,hi)=lo for x<t and hi for x>=t
263: (defaults .5,0,1); LINEAR=x; ROOT=sqrt(x); SQUARE=x*x;
264: SMOOTHSTEP=x*x*(3-2*x); SMOOTHERSTEP=x^3*(6*x*x-15*x+10);
265: SPHERE=sqrt(max(0,2*x-x*x)); POW4=x^4; INVSQUARE=x*(2-x).
266: Nonconstant default responses have endpoints 0,1. Threshold t is in [0,1],
267: including explicit endpoint behavior. TWO_STEP uses an analytic engine tag,
268: never a linearly interpolated LUT. Native distance falloff evaluates at
269: distance d; generated falloff uses x=1-d, whereas native pressure/cavity uses x.
270: Preserve hardness remapping and existing native falloff clamping from mapping.py.
271: Pressure curves may exceed [0,1]; final property range, not a curve clamp,
272: limits the result. Generated constant/step levels must be finite.
273: 
274: Switching to CUSTOM explicitly initializes an editable approximation of the
275: current preset (256 samples); discontinuous presets warn that editable Bezier
276: curves approximate the jump. Returning to a preset keeps dormant custom data;
277: returning to CUSTOM restores it. Reset/reseed is an explicit action.
278: 
279: Cache immutable LUTs by complete canonical definition and sample/domain config;
280: use hashes only as lookup accelerators. Native/custom fingerprints include all
281: evaluation-affecting points, handles, clipping, extension and mapping fields.
282: Check fingerprints at stroke start. Runtime generations accelerate known edits;
283: undo/load/deletion invalidates owner references and all engine upload state.
284: Cache capacity is 256 tables, LRU; active resolved strokes own their immutable
285: tables until completion. Track uploads separately per engine brush and command;
286: clear/rebuild invalidates uploads even when definitions match. No sampling,
287: hashing, ownership resolution or ctypes table upload occurs per dab.
288: 
289: ## Owned CurveMapping API selected for Plan 2
290: 
291: Declare `bpy.props.CurveMappingProperty(name="", description="", update=None)`.
292: v1 is a single scalar channel; no color-space variants. It is an RNA pointer
293: property whose unset read is None. Explicit
294: `owner.curve_mapping_initialize("property_name", preset='LINEAR')` allocates
295: once and returns the owned mapping; `owner.property_unset("property_name")`
296: removes it. Nesting in PropertyGroup and CollectionProperty resolves the true
297: Brush/Scene ID. `template_curve_mapping` draws an Initialize control for None
298: without allocation; its undoable first-edit operation initializes the property.
299: 
300: Persistence uses ONLY existing IDProperty GROUP/IDPARRAY/array/scalar/string
301: types: a tagged, versioned group under the declared property name. It contains
302: channel definitions, points/handles, clip/extension settings and required
303: evaluation metadata. No new IDProperty type, DNA payload, ID dependency, owner
304: pointer, sampled table or Python address is serialized. Old readers preserve
305: these ordinary properties even when the declaration is absent. Add an API
306: capability signal (`hasattr(bpy.props, 'CurveMappingProperty')`); old forks can
307: preserve data but cannot edit it with the widget. Unsupported stock readers
308: cannot run SculptCore mode; Plan 2 must prove curve-definition preservation
309: through open/resave independently of that mode capability.
310: 
311: The fork provides a runtime CurveMapping materialization of the saved group.
312: All widget/RNA mutations synchronize definitions and tag the actual owner;
313: Brush changes mark that brush's asset dirty, Scene changes tag Scene. Optional
314: Python callbacks run once after the complete mutation and are removed on
315: unregister. Essential notifications remain native and do not depend on callbacks.
316: No self-assignment of unrelated brush properties. Two declarations retain their
317: own callback identity. Read-only owners reject mutations before touching cache.
318: 
319: Raw writes to the backing IDProperty group are NOT a supported edit API.
320: After raw writes, callers must explicitly call
321: `owner.curve_mapping_sync("property_name")` before save/dirty-state inspection.
322: Sync validates the complete definition, rebuilds runtime state and notifies the
323: owner; invalid definitions raise without deleting or replacing saved data.
324: Access/evaluation can detect changes opportunistically, but raw writes alone
325: do not promise dirty propagation or save-reminder detection. File serializers
326: preserve raw data as ordinary IDProperties without callbacks or mutation.
327: When declarations are absent, raw definitions are opaque preserved properties;
328: re-register validates before use. Raw writes follow Blender's explicit undo
329: conventions. Runtime revisions are monotonic within a lifetime only.
330: 
331: Retained curve/point wrappers must carry runtime lifetime/identity tokens,
332: independent of movable arrays or IDProperty addresses. On point removal/reset,
333: owner removal, undo/load or unregister, invalidate wrappers with ReferenceError.
334: Insertion/reorder may preserve logical identity or invalidate safely. Open
335: widgets must re-resolve through an owner/record identity and close safely after
336: deletion. Do not ship raw pointers into point arrays as retained handles.
337: Copying owners deep-copies ordinary IDProperties and creates new runtime
338: identities. Revert reloads owned definitions with the Brush itself.
339: 
340: Implementation requires an owned-curve RNA property discriminator ahead of
341: generic IDProperty verification/pointer dispatch, which otherwise deletes
342: wrong-type storage or returns an IDProperty pointer as CurveMapping data.
343: Get/never-create-get, presence and unset must use this dispatch. Initialization,
344: unset and sync are native transactions: check editability, validate, invalidate
345: old handles, mutate storage, tag owner and run the declaring callback. Unknown
346: schema versions raise on edit/access but remain preserved on save.
347: 
348: Use heap-stable runtime owner records with lifetime generations checked at
349: RNA/Python access boundaries. Invalidate all affected owner-record wrappers,
350: collections, iterators and widget handles on any structural parent/point change;
351: v1 need not preserve their identity across such changes. No retained parent
352: IDProperty addresses. UI popup callbacks hold validated handles and reacquire
353: native pointers only for their immediate operation. One native commit path
354: handles RNA setters, methods and widget edits; callback once per RNA operation
355: or interactive widget update, with the enclosing drag forming one undo step.
356: Nested unset PropertyGroup lookup also needs a non-allocating path. Freeze the
357: exact tagged-group serialization schema before native code is written; its
358: supported compatibility matrix begins with the current pre-change fork and a
359: stock Blender 5.x reader, whose actual binary identity is recorded by tests.
360: The exact payload is specified in [owned-curve-storage-v1.md](owned-curve-storage-v1.md).
361: 
362: ## Commands and integration
363: 
364: Commands have explicit parent links to the resolved brush by default, optional
365: links to a named command, or no parent. Order is execution order only. Validate
366: cycles and missing parents before a stroke. Each command has sparse typed value
367: overrides and independently local/inherited whole stacks (default inherited).
368: Resolve all links on the host and upload complete typed command state without
369: inheritance flags. Existing main-plus-smooth programs retain their explicit
370: strength/invert behavior. A command without a parent uses registry defaults.
371: 
372: Evaluate command radius/extents before node selection, pinning, undo capture or
373: mutation. Use conservative union regions (all nodes for unbounded policies),
374: then execute with exactly the preflighted values. Restore authored brush and
375: command state on success, errors and cancellation. No previous-command leakage.
376: Preflight the union of topology/host-preparation requirements too. Classify
377: hooks as configuration-invariant or command-dependent; only invariant hooks
378: may be deduplicated by function pointer. Dependent hooks execute with resolved
379: command state and configuration-specific cache identity (including ENHANCE
380: rings/inner values). Test later-command cavity enable and differently configured
381: ENHANCE commands; shared-brush hook execution cannot satisfy this contract.
382: New per-command UI is outside this migration. New path remains opt-in until
383: all eight gates pass; gate tests assert which path actually executed.
